<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">A setup is not fully handed over merely because devices can connect. The household should know who administers the system, how access is recovered, who applies updates and what was checked. This guide does not collect passwords or promise a particular coverage or speed result.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="owner"><span aria-hidden="true">01</span>Identify the administrator</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Record the person or household role that administers the main router or mesh controller. Check that the system is not still attached only to an installer’s account, a former resident’s account or an administrator nobody can reach.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Also record which device is the main network device, where its current documentation is kept and what the household expects to happen if the administrator changes. Enter credentials privately in the equipment’s supported process; do not place them in a shared checklist or send them to someone helping.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="records"><span aria-hidden="true">02</span>Keep recovery and update records</h2>
<div class="mb-8 overflow-x-auto" role="region" aria-label="New home Wi-Fi handover records" tabindex="0"><table role="table" class="w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm md:text-base"><thead><tr role="row"><th scope="col" class="border border-line bg-surface p-4 font-bold text-ink">Record</th><th scope="col" class="border border-line bg-surface p-4 font-bold text-ink">Keep this context</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr role="row"><td role="cell" data-label="Record" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">Equipment</td><td role="cell" data-label="Keep this context" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">Model, hardware details and date added</td></tr>
<tr role="row"><td role="cell" data-label="Record" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">Ownership</td><td role="cell" data-label="Keep this context" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">Administrator and update responsibility</td></tr>
<tr role="row"><td role="cell" data-label="Record" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">Recovery</td><td role="cell" data-label="Keep this context" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">Where the private recovery process is documented</td></tr>
<tr role="row"><td role="cell" data-label="Record" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">Changes</td><td role="cell" data-label="Keep this context" class="border border-line p-4 align-top text-muted">What changed and what was checked afterward</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
{% call note("care", "Take care", "A reset can remove configuration") %}<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Before resetting or replacing equipment, make sure the household knows how it will regain administrator access and reconnect essential devices. Keep recovery codes and passwords in the household’s secure system, not in this article’s worksheet.</p>{% endcall %}
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">For example, Google warns that a <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.google.com/googlehome/answer/6246619?hl=en">factory reset erases its Wi-Fi devices’ settings and requires setup again</a>. This is a reason to check the recovery plan, not an instruction to reset working equipment.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="guest"><span aria-hidden="true">03</span>Check guest access</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If visitors or temporary devices need Wi-Fi, check whether the equipment supports a guest network and what separation it provides. Read the current documentation for whether guest devices can reach household devices, whether access can be limited or turned off, which nodes provide it, and whether the setting survives an update or reset.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Do not assume every guest network behaves the same way. The household should enter its own credentials privately and decide who is allowed to change guest access.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Google’s <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.google.com/googlehome/answer/6327302?hl=en">guest Wi-Fi documentation</a>, for example, allows selected household devices to be shared with guests. A “guest” label therefore does not establish that all household devices are inaccessible; check the exact product and settings.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="check"><span aria-hidden="true">04</span>Check the handover</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Ask the administrator to demonstrate, without sharing a password, which device is the main controller, where the documentation is kept, how updates are handled and where recovery information is securely managed. Record any item that remains unknown rather than treating the setup as complete.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">A successful connection proves only that a device connected at that moment. It does not prove that administrator ownership, recovery, updates or guest separation are ready.</p>
